      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "This study aimed to clarify the determinants of ST-segment level in lead aVR in anterior wall acute myocardial infarction (AAMI)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We analyzed ST-segment levels in all 12 leads on admission and emergency coronary angiographic findings in 261 patients with a first AAMI with ST-segment elevation. The length of the left anterior descending coronary artery (LAD) was classified as follows: short = not reaching the apex; medium = perfusing less than 25% of the inferior wall; long = perfusing 25% or more of the inferior wall."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The ST-segment level in lead aVR correlated significantly with the ST-segment levels in leads I, II, III, aVF, V(1), and V(3-6), especially with those in leads II and V(6) (r = -0.63, P < .001; r = -0.61, P < .001; respectively). Patients with a proximal LAD occlusion had a greater ST-segment level in lead aVR than those with a distal LAD occlusion (P < .001). Patients with a long LAD had a lower ST-segment level than those with a short or medium LAD (P < .05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The ST-segment levels, especially in leads II and V(6), the site of the LAD occlusion, and the length of the LAD affect the ST-segment level in lead aVR in ST-segment elevation AAMI."
        }
      ]},
